this is tomorrow same standing in for  reviewer Mike Epstein neo2 is not to be  trifled with building on the originals  tough-as-nails reputation team ninja  second samurai action RPG brings back  the originals partial for punishing and  highly nuanced combat the sequel hones  in on the originals distinctive take on  the souls like without completely  reinventing itself the result is a long  tough slog that will push even the most  challenged hungry players to their  breaking points despite the title neo 2  is a prequel revealing the secret  history of a decades-long period of war  in medieval Japan the story gives just  enough context for you to follow along  and make you feel like you're making  progress without getting in the way of  gameplay with core mechanics refined  from the bones of Dark Souls neo to  balls down to a series of battles and  deals in all kinds of situations these  battles demand intense precision not  only are your attacks and skills limited  by a stamina meter called ki for any  extra attack or mistimed movement will  leave you exposed often to an attack  that will cost you a substantial amount  of health like other souls like games  there's a painful pleasure in mastering  whatever opponents the game throws your  way  neo - builds on the wonderfully diverse  range of options for developing a  personal fighting style the original  systems return and the core weapon  combat remains largely unchanged from  the original beyond some new abilities  and two new weapon types the speedy  two-handed switch clave and really  speedy double hatchets neo 2 demands  that you have profound understanding of  all the attacks your weapons can perform  but there's a wide range of attacks and  they each put their own spin on how you  fight neo twos biggest additions revolve  around the idea that he can channel  yokai spirits the most important is a  hard parry called the burst counter  which allows you to count a powerful  enemy attacks every enemy has at least  one attack that's vulnerable to the  counter they're often big powerful moves  that you'll be tempted to dodge fighting  the urge and throwing yourself at your  enemy to turn the tide of battle for a  moment is crucial which makes the combat  feel more tactical and aggressive in the  moment when you spot an enemy prepping a  burst attack you feel successful like  you've gotten one over on your opponent  even for a second  because the game is so difficult these  little victories help drive you forward  [Music]  you learn yokai abilities by equippable  soul cause that allow you to momentarily  transform into the enemies you've killed  to use one of their attacks more than  ninjutsu and magic which returned from  the original game spirit cause add a  much wider range of contextually useful  skills for example as the monkey yokai  Enki you jump into the air and throw a  spear which is quite novel as neo2  doesn't have a jump on when the yokai  get bigger  sometimes a giant head or fist or foot  magically appears to maim your enemies  they aren't so powerful that you can  lean on them to win a fight but these  skills widely expand the range of things  you can potentially do last but not  least neo - adds a super powerful yokai  shift transformation which temporarily  makes you faster and stronger triggering  the transformation does not eliminate  the need for tactics though you are  invulnerable of using attacks and taking  damage reduce the amount of time you  have in your stronger form a failed  assault in yokai mode not only wastes a  powerful slowly charging asset but may  also leave you unexpectedly exposed if  you revert to your old self because your  opponent caught you off-guard in true  neo fashion even your greatest strength  can become a chance for your enemy to  get the upper hand  learning your own skill set is just part  of the experience to really excel you  also need to understand neo two's wide  world there's an astounding amount of  variety across an extremely long  campaign it's winding multi area  missions span all kinds of environments  from burning castles and temples to  military camps to forests and mountain  sides many of them change radically as  you explore them giving you a sense of  travel and accomplishment for covering  what feels like a long distance one  early level for example starts on a  hillside outside a castle and ends in a  massive underground cave even when the  levels seem similar very design makes  each one feel distinct and worth  conquering it helps that the maps are  more than twisty turny dungeon crawls  most have at least one area with a  unique trap or environmental conundrum  in one forest level for instance a giant  owl youkai patrols certain areas  alerting enemies if it sees you also  there are dark realm zones black and  white areas haunted by yokai that  provide an EVA greater challenge by  slowing down your key generation it's  only by defeating us first  the enemy in a dark room that it will  dispel permanently injecting more ways  for you to make progress that doesn't  reset when you use a shrine or die even  for all its variety neo2 stretches all  of its content as much as it can for  every mission in its core campaign there  are two or three side missions many of  which remix a portion of the story  mission Plus upon finishing the campaign  you'll get access to a difficulty level  with higher-level enemies and gear while  it can be a little annoying in principle  to play the same section of a level  three to four times each version finds  little ways to change your path and  present new challenges to keep things  fresh  likewise neo 2 never seems to run out of  new enemies to throw at you almost every  level has at least one new type of  youkai for you to study and struggle  against each enemy has its own range of  a Balise and you need to learn  everything about them in order to  anticipate their attacks and get the  upper hand the process takes time you  won't get it on the first try or even  after the first victory every enemy can  kill you if you aren't bringing your  a-game dissecting enemy patterns and  figuring out how to counter them is the  sweetest pleasure neo - offers that  there are so many enemies with so many  different attacks to navigate ensures  that the game never loses flavor you see  this most clearly when you go up against  each of the game's extraordinarily  difficult boss encounters like the  levels the bosses vary wildly and are  all sites to behold from a giant snake  with many snake arms to a three-story  spider with a Bulls head each flagship  enemy design has a lot of character and  is unlike anything you've seen in the  game before they all have one thing in  common though they're extremely  difficult even more than standard vowels  the bosses effectively demand perfect  play for an extended period you need to  be able to recognize every move they  make as they make it and know how to  respond instantly very few took me less  than a dozen tries and many of them took  multiple hours at times I wondered if  maybe some of these encounters should be  a little short as there were many bosses  where I felt I had mastered their  pattern but couldn't finish because they  landed a single one hit kill late in the  fight ultimately that excruciating  difficulty and the feeling evokes our  baked into neo twos DNA and his boss  fights remain compelling even as they  vex and frustrate though the difficulty  can sometimes  feel like a curse upon you as you play  it's a testament that neo to  successfully grabs and holds your  complete attention so close for so long  [Music]  [Music]
 

